{"assets":["/editorial-assets/ac1faba564374af52d25d5570960692f3930937396570c5a84de30a0e641bcdb.png","/editorial-assets/8db3915f19f29d5afbc0e6faa61b30dc042838d02fc19de3f78046ce01645167.png","/editorial-assets/ee4e1fc76c17e4611f8d1aa06bc9d3ff53cf3b18b9ac24326166b570766b2731.png","/editorial-assets/251e06c4d8a30abe45e1a691e52fe5acdf978dfc4bea7a11ccb6e5c344e494a5.png","/editorial-assets/d4ce9f007b0f74842e6bd60512e52d7840c4cdd0e67d011a3efc38f7b18993a7.png"],"contestId":"283","diagnostics":[],"document":{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Here is the editorial for Round #174. Thanks for participating. We hope you enjoyed the problems! :)"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Div2 A"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"We didn’t expect this problem to be so hard :(. This problem can be solved by brute forcing. For any "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":",  you can compute "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/ac1faba564374af52d25d5570960692f3930937396570c5a84de30a0e641bcdb.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":" in "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"O"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") time (iteratively multiply cur = (cur * i) % p, not use pow in math library!), so overall brute force will be "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"O"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"2"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":") time."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Note: there is actually "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/8db3915f19f29d5afbc0e6faa61b30dc042838d02fc19de3f78046ce01645167.png"},"kind":"image"},{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/blog/entry/7037"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"algorithm."}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"The problem was written by abacadaea."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Div2 B"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"We first note that players who have folded do not affect our desired answer. Then, we can do casework on the number of players who are currently “IN”. If no cows are “IN”, then all the players who are “ALLIN” can show their hands. If exactly one cow is “IN”, she is the only one who can show, so the answer is 1. If two or more cows are “IN”, no one can show their hands. Then we simply count the number of cows of each type and check for each case. The total runtime is "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"O"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":")."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"The problem was written by scott_wu."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Div1 A / Div2 C Consider the problem with only queries 1 and 2. Then the problem is easy in "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"O"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"): keep track of the number of terms and the sum, and you can handle each query in O(1). But with query 3 we need to also be able to find the last term of the sequence at any given time. To do this, we keep track of the sequence "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"d"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" = "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + 1"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" - "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" for "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" = 1, 2, ..., "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"s"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - 1,  and "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"s"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":",  where "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"s"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" is the length of the sequence. Notice that query 2 only modifies one value of "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"d"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":",  and queries 1 and 3 are easily processed and able to update this information. This gives us an "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"O"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") algorithm."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"One can also use a fenwick or segment tree to compute the last element, but it’s not nearly as nice :)."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"The problem was written by abacadaea."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Div1 B / Div2 D"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"First, suppose we only have the sequence "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"2"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"3"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":", …"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":". We note that the current state is only determined by the location and the direction we are facing, so there are only 2·("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - 1) states total. Then, we can use DFS with memorization to find the distance traveled from each state, or  - 1 if a cycle is formed, in "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"O"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") time. Now, when we add "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"1"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" into the sequence, we essentially only need to give the distance traveled starting from each state facing left. The only difference is that if we ever land on "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"1"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" again, there must be a cycle, as we started on "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"1"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":". Using this, we can solve the problem in "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"O"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") time total."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"The problem was written by scott_wu."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Div1 C / Div2 E"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Imagine the problem as a graph where coins are the nodes and Bessie’s statements are directed edges between coins. Because of the problem conditions, the graph must be a set of cycles and directed paths. If there are any cycles in the graph, the answer is clearly 0."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Then, suppose we have a path "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"1"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"2"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":", …"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" in the graph, where it is known that we have more coins of type "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"1"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" than of type "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"2"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":", more of type "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"2"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" than of type "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"3"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":",  and so on. The key observation in this problem is that this is equivalent to having k independent coins of value {"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"1"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":"), "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"1"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":") + "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"2"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":"), "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"1"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":") + "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"2"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":") + "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"3"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":"), …}. The first coin in our new list represents how many more coins of type "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"1"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" than of type "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"2"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" we have, the second coin in our new list represents how many more coins of type "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"2"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" than of type "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"3"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" we have, and so on. However, we must be careful to note that we need at least one of each of the new coins except for the last one, so we can subtract their values from T before doing the DP."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"After creating our new set of values, we can run the DP the same way we would run a st